{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"just-preglacial-yeti","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\nPhase 1 (`darkish-enormous-raptors`, `76b2-4b5f-5682-45e5`) removed **29,931** duplicate\nbridge-echo comments across 30 Jira issues, identified by the reconciler marker\n`RECONCILER_MARKER = \"<!-- rebar:reconciler-echo -->\"` on the pre-strip rendered ADF text.\n\nIt deliberately **skipped 836 comments** across three issues — **REB-1567 (418), REB-1931 (278),\nREB-2605 (140)** — because they carry no marker and therefore failed phase 1's authoritative\nidentifier. That restraint was correct: the identifying rule for them is inferred, not the\nprecedent's, and deletion is irreversible.\n\nThey are not human comments. Each is the **same bot accountId** as phase 1\n(`712020:6471376f-…`), appears in roughly 139 identical copies per issue, is **exactly 32767\nrendered characters**, and ends in the reconciler's own `_TRUNCATION_SUFFIX`.\n\n## Why the marker is missing — a real defect, filed separately\n\n`unreal-empathetic-illadopsis` (`5931-78a3-95d2-44cd`): outbound truncation appends the marker\n**after** the body, so the 32767-char cut removes it. The loop-breaker is therefore already inert\nfor any over-length comment — which is exactly why these three issues duplicated despite phase 1's\nmechanism existing.\n\n## Scope: this ticket is the CLEANUP only\n\nFixing the truncation-order defect belongs to `5931`. This ticket removes the duplicates that\ndefect already produced.\n\n**Deliberately NOT folded into `76b2`.** That ticket's plan and acceptance criteria describe the\nmarker-based cleanup; its plan-review attestation covers that plan. Widening it to absorb a\ndifferent identifying rule would be a material scope change to a reviewed plan — the failure mode\n`bediasite-bilineate-stoat` documents.\n\n## Identification rule — all four conditions, conjunctive\n\nA comment is a candidate ONLY if every one holds:\n\n1. author accountId == the phase-1 bot accountId;\n2. rendered text length == **exactly 32767**;\n3. rendered text ends with `_TRUNCATION_SUFFIX`;\n4. the comment carries **no** `RECONCILER_MARKER` (marker-bearing comments were phase 1's business).\n\nAny comment failing ANY condition is not a candidate and is never deleted.\n\nThe tightened rule is already implemented in `/Users/joeoakhart/jira-cleanup-20260820/cleanup.py`\nbehind `--include-truncated`; the exact-length check (condition 2) was added after the earlier dry\nrun, so **the previously observed REB-2605 figure (145 -> 7 kept, 138 deletions) is an UPPER BOUND**\nand must be re-confirmed by a fresh dry run before any execution.\n\n## Sequencing hazard — read before executing\n\n`_record_comment_id` records a comment id **only on a successful new post**; it does not backfill\nids for comments already in Jira (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:311-333`). So on the FIRST bridge pass\nafter re-enabling, these comments have no recorded id, the PRIMARY id-identity skip cannot fire,\nand the SECONDARY body-equality skip cannot match them (their bodies were truncated). **They will be\nre-posted once, then deduped from pass 2 onward.**\n\nCleaning before the bridge is re-enabled therefore does not durably remove them. The operator's\ninstruction is to clean first, then re-enable; this ticket follows that order and records the\nconsequence explicitly so the re-post is expected rather than mistaken for a regression, and so a\nfinal sweep after the first pass is planned rather than improvised.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A fresh dry run under the four-condition rule is produced and inspected BEFORE any deletion,\n      and its candidate count is recorded per issue.\n- [ ] Pre-deletion backup of the full comment payload for each affected issue exists before any\n      DELETE, gated on `len(fetched) == total`.\n- [ ] Canary: execute on the smallest issue (REB-2605) alone, verify it fully, before touching the\n      other two.\n- [ ] **Keep-one holds**: for each distinct normalized body the OLDEST copy survives; a body with a\n      single copy is never deleted — verified per issue by re-fetching and asserting every distinct\n      body present before is present after.\n- [ ] Non-candidate comments are untouched — verified by comparing non-candidate counts before and\n      after.\n- [ ] Ambiguous cases are SKIPPED and reported, never guessed.\n- [ ] The observed population matches the expected ~836 across exactly those three issues; a\n      material divergence stops execution and is reported instead.","id":"aac0-dbdf-57ac-4fff","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Phase 2 Jira cleanup: remove truncated bridge-echo duplicates that carry no reconciler marker"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787272695027424001,"uuid":"9fdc1afe-a830-4800-b6b4-bd93af107862"}